# Keep shell scripts LF-only.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: this repo is edited on Windows but almost every script is
# executed on Linux (the H100 pods, the T4 box, the A100). Git's autocrlf would
# hand those scripts back with CRLF endings, and Linux then fails them with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" -- or, more insidiously, keeps a trailing \r
# inside variables so paths and comparisons silently misbehave.
#
# This is the same CRLF trap DEPLOY_H100_POD.md documents for the Windows
# tar-pipe, where it had to be re-discovered on BOTH 07/07 and 07/17. Pinning it
# here stops it recurring by way of a plain `git clone`.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Python is likewise run on the servers.
*.py text eol=lf

# Keep data/artefact formats byte-stable.
*.jsonl -text
*.json text eol=lf
*.tar.gz binary
*.png binary
*.pdf binary

# ★ ALL .txt are consumed on the Linux servers (datasets, eval-gold references,
# few-shot fixtures, prompt templates). git STORES them LF, but core.autocrlf on
# Windows hands them back CRLF, and the Windows tar-pipe then carries that \r to
# the pods. A trailing \r there does two kinds of silent damage:
#   1) it rides into model input, and
#   2) it rides into the eval GOLD -- the tail entity of every reference triple
#      gets a \r, so NO strict full-triple match lands and strict/exact/partial
#      collapse to 0.000 while B-cubed (which normalises) reads a healthy ~0.75.
# That exact signature cost a backbone webnlg run on 2026-07-20 (DECISIONS
# 2026-07-20b; DEPLOY_H100_POD.md 5a). Pin them LF so a plain clone is safe.
*.txt text eol=lf
